FILE TYPES - 
1.  CSV (Comma Separated Values) - A plain text file where data is stored row by row, separated by commas.
    🔹 How data is stored
    id,name,dept
    1,Arun,IT
    2,Ravi,HR

    👉 Stored as:
    Row-wise
    No schema (just text)
    No data types (everything is string internally)

    🔹 Pros
    ✔ Simple
    ✔ Easy to read
    ✔ Supported everywhere

    🔹 Cons
    ❌ No compression (large size)
    ❌ Slow for big data
    ❌ No schema enforcement
    ❌ No support for nested data

2. JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) - A semi-structured format used for nested and hierarchical data

    🔹 How data is stored
    {
    "id": 1,
    "name": "Arun",
    "skills": ["Python", "SQL"]
    }

    👉 Stored as:
    Key-value pairs
    Supports nested structures (arrays, objects)

    🔹 Pros
    ✔ Handles nested data
    ✔ Flexible schema
    ✔ Widely used in APIs

    🔹 Cons
    ❌ Large file size
    ❌ Slow processing
    ❌ Not optimized for analytics

3. PARQUET - A columnar storage format optimized for big data processing
    
    🔹 How data is stored
    Column 1 (id):   1, 2
    Column 2 (name): Arun, Ravi
    Column 3 (dept): IT, HR

    🔹 Pros
    ✔ Very fast (column pruning)
    ✔ High compression (less storage)
    ✔ Schema stored with data
    ✔ Best for analytics (Spark, Databricks)

    🔹 Cons
    ❌ Not human-readable
    ❌ Slightly complex

🔹 Why Parquet is best
✔ Reads only required columns -- because its is columnar storage
✔ Less I/O [Less data read from storage (ADLS/S3)]→ faster queries
✔ Better compression → lower cost
✔ Works perfectly with Spark

Real Data Engineering Example
👉 Table has 50 columns
👉 Query uses only 3 columns
Row format → reads all 50 ❌
Columnar → reads only 3 ✅
👉 That’s why Parquet is used in Databricks (Bronze → Silver → Gold)